;
; Handoff — carrier generation, OOK gating and self-measurement. M3, M13.
; design §10.1, §6.3, §9.8; development plan M3, M13.
;
; TWO STATE MACHINES ON ONE PAD, which is what lets M3 measure itself with no
; external hardware at all: the PIO input mux reads the pad independently of
; whichever peripheral is driving it, so the counter below sees the carrier the
; generator above is producing without a jumper, a scope, or a second board.
;

;
; carrier_out — one pad LEVEL and one pad DIRECTION per half-period.
;
; Each half-period is a SLOT of three state-machine cycles and two stream
; bits: the first bit is the pad level, the second is whether the pad is
; driven at all. A mark chip is slots (1,drive) (0,drive) (1,drive) ... ; a
; space chip is slots (x,release) — the pad is high-impedance for the whole
; chip, not driven low. design §9.8 has the numbers: a driven-low space pulls
; the wristband's own stage-1 bias to 0.28 V and C1/R6 then take ~17 ms to
; come back, against a 1 ms turnaround budget; a released pad sits at VREF
; and there is nothing to recover from.
;
; Three cycles rather than two because the half-period must be a whole number
; of system cycles: 150 MHz / (2 x 200 kHz) = 375 = 3 x 125, and the 40 kHz
; bring-up carrier gives 1875 = 3 x 625. Two cycles would need 187.5. config.h
; static-asserts the division.
;
; This is deliberately a bit stream rather than a counted toggle loop. A loop
; that pulls a chip and then toggles N times spends different numbers of cycles
; on the mark path and the space path unless every branch is hand-balanced, and
; an unbalanced branch shifts every chip after the first gated one — which is
; precisely what M3's third exit criterion ("gating on and off is chip-aligned")
; exists to catch. With one slot per two bits the alignment is a property of
; the data, not of the control flow, and there is nothing left to get wrong.
; The pad direction is in the same stream for the same reason: it switches on
; exactly the slot the data says, never a few cycles later.
;
; Autopull refills the OSR, so a starved FIFO stalls the pad in its last slot
; rather than emitting a half chip; every send ends in an explicit released
; slot so that last slot is high-impedance.
;
.program carrier_out
.wrap_target
    out pins, 1
    out pindirs, 1  [1]
.wrap

;
; edge_count — count rising edges on the same pad.
;
; x is preloaded with 0xFFFFFFFF by the CPU and decremented once per rising
; edge, so the count is 0xFFFFFFFF minus whatever x holds when the gate closes.
; Waiting for the low first means a pad that is already high when the gate
; opens cannot contribute a spurious first edge.
;
; Clocked at full system speed: at 200 kHz the pad changes every 375 cycles, so
; there is no question of missing one.
;
.program edge_count
count:
    wait 0 pin 0
    wait 1 pin 0
    jmp x--, count
